          "text": "CUDA graph replay is faster than eager kernel launching primarily because it eliminates the significant CPU-side overhead associated with the launch process. In eager execution, every kernel launch requires the CPU to validate arguments, manage stream dependencies, and communicate with the GPU driver, which can take several microseconds per launch. For applications with many small kernels, this CPU overhead can dominate execution time, leaving the GPU idle while waiting for instructions. CUDA graphs capture the entire sequence of operations\u2014including kernels, memory copies, and dependencies\u2014into a static data structure on the GPU. When replayed, the GPU executes this pre-compiled graph with minimal CPU intervention, drastically reducing launch latency and allowing for tighter, more efficient scheduling of tasks.\n\nHowever, this performance gain comes with specific costs and constraints. The primary cost is the upfront overhead of capturing and instantiating the graph, which can be expensive and is only amortized if the graph is replayed many times. Additionally, CUDA graphs are static; once captured, the structure of the graph (the sequence of operations and their dependencies) cannot change. This means that if your application requires dynamic control flow, varying kernel parameters, or changing memory addresses between iterations, you must either use complex workarounds (like updating pointers via `cudaGraphExecKernelNodeSetParams`)",
